"Boudewijn Waijers" <kroisos@REMOVETHIS.home.nl> writes:

> Igor D. WonderLlama wrote:
> 
> > I agree with David here.  The point is not to make the easy game
> > harder.  The point is to make the dificulty more consistent.
> 
> A simple way to accomplish this is to disallow the creation of powerful
> wands on the lower levels. For example, don't create wands of wishing,
> polymorph, and death on levels 1-5. Bones creation already works this way
> (no bones for levels 1-3).
> 
> However, this would radically change the philosophy behind NetHack. In
> NetHack, all items have the same generation chance per item class on all
> dungeon levels (the distribution between the various item classes may
> differ per dungeon branch).
> 
> Tweaking the generation chance on a per-level basis would turn NetHack
> into Moria, where items are only created on an "appropriate" depth (but
> sometimes not).

I don't think that adopting one feature of another rogue game 
would make the two games the same.  However, I am also against 
the min_level idea; people could e.g. start diving down, hoping 
for good stuff, then rushing back up.  Another way to tweak this 
would be to make wands of wishing less reliable.  Maybe the wand 
was worked out on a friday, maybe the wish receiver unit is 
broken.  So, sometimes when you wish for grey dragon scale mail, 
you just get a (grey ;-)) scale mail, or maybe a grey dragon.  
Or the wand gives your wish item to a random monster 10 levels 
below, since the 3d-give_it unit malfunctions.  You could also 
just get a cheap plastic imitation of your wished item since 
it is out of stock at the moment.  Something along these lines, 
maybe.  Some bad things should possibly happen, against which 
you would be able to prepare yourself until later midgame.  

However, I believe the DevTeam likes early wishes, just to 
give the newer players a feeling of success here and there. 
 
